  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThrough Wed...Pressure gradient will gradually relax as low\npressure developing off the NC coast lifts NE. This will allow the\nNE winds to ease a bit but remain breezy/gusty esp along the\ncoast. Some drier air overspreads the area Tue which will limit\nthe amount of showers that push onshore but there will still be a\nfew. But low levels moisten again on Wednesday, so expect to see\nsome increase in coverage of Atlc showers beginning Wed aftn and\ninto Thu esp south of the Cape. Max temps should tick up a degree\nor two with a little more heating/less clouds Tue. Low temps Tue\nand Wed mornings could reach the upper 50s north of I4. We'll see\nif Orlando (MCO) can dip below 60F one of these mornings for the\nfirst time this season. \n\nA High Surf Advisory and high risk for life-threatening rip currents \nwill continue Tuesday, and the high risk for rip currents persists \nfurther into Wednesday as well. Entering the water is strongly \ndiscouraged! \n\nThu-Sun...Models are developing another low pressure near or to \nthe east of the Bahamas Fri-Sat along an old frontal boundary. \nThis tightens the pressure gradient and increases the NE winds, \nsetting up for a windy weekend, potentially very windy. Both GFS \nand ECMWF show pressure difference of 10 mb between JAX-MIA which\nwould produce sustained winds solidly in Wind Adv criteria. Also,\nadditional rough, pounding surf will produce more beach erosion. \nHave low confidence on the high rain chances depicted Sat-Sun but\nat a minimum there will be scattered Atlc showers pushing onshore,\nlikely very fast-moving. \n\nDaytime temperatures will change little, holding in the upper 70s\nto low 80s, with milder overnight lows due to stronger onshore \nflow Friday onward.",
